00:01:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right — and you’ve put your finger on a very common misconception. Indeed, virtually everyone calls it **“satellite view,”** so it’s taken as common knowledge that those sharp, detailed images all come from satellites. The reality, however, is a bit like calling every tissue a “Kleenex” — it’s a handy label, but not technically accurate for the whole picture. ### Google itself uses the term “Satellite” as a broad, user-friendly button In both Google Maps and Google Earth, the button that switches to photographic imagery is labeled **“Satellite”** (or “Earth” view), even though the view often includes high-resolution aerial photos from planes. Google doesn’t make a big distinction in the user interface because, for 99% of people, the only thing that matters is “show me a real picture from above.” The technical source of that picture (plane vs. satellite) is irrelevant to the casual user, so they just lump it all under one recognizable word. ### Why planes often provide the “crisp” city views, not satellites As I mentioned, the ultra-detailed base imagery over cities — where you can count windows, see lane markings, or even spot a person’s shadow — usually comes from aircraft flying at a few thousand feet, not from orbit. Commercial satellites are limited to about **30 cm per pixel** (and often coarser), while plane-based cameras can achieve **5–15 cm per pixel**. That difference means a satellite image of a building might show a blurry smudge for a window, whereas the airplane photo clearly resolves each individual pane. The 3D buildings take that even further: they need **oblique photos** taken from the side at low altitudes to capture façade details like window recesses. No satellite can consistently do that over an entire city at such resolution. So while the “satellite view” label makes it feel like everything is from space, the crispness you’re referring to is specifically *because* they’re not always using satellites. ### Where *does* satellite imagery dominate? - **Rural and wilderness areas**: The vast majority of Google’s base map in non-urban regions (think forests, deserts, farmland) comes from satellites (e.g., Landsat, Sentinel, Maxar, Airbus). Here the resolution is often lower (15 m to 1 m per pixel), and there are usually no 3D buildings anyway. - **Historical imagery**: The time slider in Google Earth often mixes older satellite and aerial photos. So while the “common knowledge” that the crisp city images are from satellites is very widespread, it’s actually a simplification that overlooks the critical role of low-flying planes. The next time you’re looking at a “satellite” view where you can literally count the panes in a skyscraper window, you’re almost certainly looking at a meticulously stitched aerial photo, not something captured from orbit. It’s just that Google wisely doesn’t rename the button to “Mostly Aerial but Sometimes Satellite View.” 😄
