00:05:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a completely fair point, and I totally understand why your own experience convinces you it’s satellites doing the work. After all, you click the **Satellite** button, you zoom into your house, and it’s crisp. That’s a firsthand, repeated observation—so of course it feels like satellites are the direct source. But here’s the gentle twist: **what you’ve been looking at under that “Satellite” button might very well be an airplane photo, and Google simply doesn’t tell you that.** ### The “Satellite” button is a user‑interface label, not a promise of the image source In both Google Maps and Google Earth, the layer that shows photographic imagery is called **Satellite** for simplicity—because it’s the common term people use. But that layer is actually a seamless mosaic stitched together from many sources: - Satellites (Maxar, Airbus) for broad coverage and rural areas. - Airplanes for high‑detail urban and suburban areas. - Sometimes even drones for very small, hyper‑local projects. When you zoom into your house in a developed country, especially in North America, Europe, or parts of East Asia, it’s overwhelmingly likely that the imagery is from a **low‑flying airplane**. Google simply places that crisp aerial photo into the same “Satellite” button layer without a label that says “Airplane view.” So from your perspective, you click Satellite, you see a crisp image—and you naturally, and reasonably, conclude “satellites did this.” But the UI has just merged two very different things under one friendly name. ### How can you tell it’s a plane photo, even in your own neighborhood? There are a few giveaways, even without inside knowledge: 1. **Resolution details** – If you can clearly see the gap between cars in a driveway, the shadow of a basketball hoop, or individual shingles on your roof, that’s **better than 15 cm per pixel**. Commercial satellite tops out at 30 cm, and at that scale, a car is a blob, window frames are fuzzy. So the crispness you’re describing points straight to an airplane. 2. **Oblique views in 3D** – If you tilt Google Earth and see the side of your house with textures, windows, and maybe even the garage door’s panel lines, that’s **impossible from a satellite**. Satellites can’t see vertical walls. The fact that this works for your house means Google flew a plane over your neighborhood at some point. 3. **Image credits (on desktop Google Earth Pro)** – If you open the same location in the desktop version of Google Earth Pro, the copyright line often says something like “Sanborn”, “Aerodata”, “Hexagon”, or “Maxar” (but Maxar also has an aerial division, confusingly). Many suburbs are covered by **Pictometry** or **Nearmap** aerial imagery licensed to Google. Next time you’re at a computer, check—you might be surprised to see a name that isn’t a satellite operator. 4. **Update frequency** – In many suburban areas, the imagery gets refreshed every 1–3 years. Some of those updates are from planes flown specifically for local government or commercial mapping, then sold to Google. Satellites tend to update on a slower, global schedule. ### “But it’s been crisp every single time I’ve checked” — that actually supports the plane explanation Think about it: if your house were covered solely by commercial satellite photos, you’d notice the image is good but a little soft. The fact that it’s consistently sharp across different dates (assuming you’ve looked over the years) suggests that a plane has been your area’s regular photographer, and Google has been renewing that aerial imagery. It’s not a hidden secret; it’s just that there’s no “Airplane” button, so the plane’s work is always presented under the “Satellite” label. ### So does your personal experience contradict what I’ve been saying? No—it actually aligns perfectly with the “hidden plane thing.” Your house, and likely your friends’ houses, are in neighborhoods where the sharp, window‑counting imagery comes from aircraft, just like the downtown skyscrapers. The only difference is that you don’t see a “Plane View” button; you just see the result under “Satellite.” The technology behind the scenes is a mix, but when you’re seeing the crisp detail you’re describing, the airplane is almost certainly the cameraman, and the “Satellite” label is just the app’s convenient, if misleading, name for the photographic layer. I think that once you start checking the credits or looking at the extreme detail in 3D mode, you’ll see that the hidden plane thing has been hiding in plain sight all along—under the “Satellite” button. Your personal experience is completely valid; it’s just that the label tricked you (and millions of others) into thinking the camera is in orbit, when it’s really just a few thousand feet above your roof.
